r \"Half Flush\" (6 points) with one suit + honors. My hand has tiles from all three suits and a dragon, so \"All Types\" is a possibility if I can form melds. But I need to focus on building melds efficiently.\n\nPromising discards:\n- **Play B4**: B4 is a middle tile, not terminal, not honor. It's relatively safe (B4 unshown:3, so many left). It doesn't fit well with my B1,B3 (B1 is terminal, B3 is simple). Discarding B4 reduces Dots suit, possibly aiming for \"One Voided Suit\" (1 point) later.\n- **Play T2**: T2 is simple, but Bamboos have potential for chows with T3,T5. T2 could be part of a Chow [T1,T2,T3] or [T2,T3,T4]. But I have T3 twice, so T2 is less critical. Unshown T2:2, so some left.\n- **Play W1**: W1 is terminal, harder to use in chows, but could be part of a pung. Unshown W1:2, so two left. Discarding it might simplify hand.\n- **Play J2**: J2 is dragon. Keeping it might allow Dragon Pung (2 points) if I draw another. But discarding it might be safe since previous player has F3 pung, not dragons. Unshown J2:4 (but I hold 2, so 2 left in wall). However, J2 is a potential point source.\n\nReason: I should aim to build a hand with scoring potential while considering safety. Currently, my hand lacks coherence. Discarding a tile that is less useful for common patterns and safer is prudent. B4 is a simple tile, not closely connected to my other tiles (B1 is terminal, B3 is simple but B4 doesn't form a sequence with B1; B3,B4 could become a Chow with B2 or B5, but I don't have B2 or B5). Also, discarding B4 avoids reducing chances for sequences in Characters or Bamboos, where I have more tiles. B4 is relatively safe based on discard history (only one B4 discarded by next player). Thus, discard B4 to start shaping the hand.\n\nAnswer: Play B4"}
